Now consider nums where descending might not place all bit14-set first? But descending naturally puts larger numbers first, and larger numbers tend to have higher bits set, but not always: e.g., 2^15 - 1 = 32767 is all bits set, small number like 16 = 0b10000 has bit4 set, 15 = 0b1111 has bits 0-3 set, so 16 > 15, descending puts 16 before 15, which is good since bit4 > bit3

Yes, descending order by value should prioritize higher bits, because if a number has bit k set and another has only bits < k set, then the one with bit k set is at least 2^k, while the other is < 2^k, so descending order puts it first.

Formally, if x has bit k set and y has all bits < k set (i.e., y < 2^k), and x ≥ 2^k (since bit k set), then x ≥ 2^k > y (since y < 2^k), so x > y in value.

Thus descending sort by value will place all numbers with bit k set before numbers with only bits < k set, for the highest k where any bit differs.

This ensures that for the MSB that appears in nums, all numbers with that MSB come first.

More precisely, let k_max be the maximum bit position set in any nums (k_max ≤ 14).

All nums with bit k_max set will be ≥ 2^k_max, and all nums without bit k_max set are < 2^k_max, so descending sort puts all bit k_max set numbers before non-bit k_max set numbers.

Similarly, among bit k_max set numbers, those with bit k_max-1 set might be >= 2^{k_max-1}, but since they already have 2^k_max, value comparison doesn't directly enforce bit k_max-1 order, but within bit k_max set numbers, larger values tend to have more higher bits set, but might skip bits.

